<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Charles II, Duke of Guelders</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">1467–1538</span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a> · Also known as Charles of Egmond</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Charles II, Duke of Guelders, known as Charles of Egmond, recovered the duchy his family had lost to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-the-bold">Charles the Bold</a> and fought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> for over four decades with French support. He extended his power over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/groningen">Groningen</a>, Friesland and Utrecht. Unable to keep these gains, he left Guelders to William of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kleve">Cleves</a>, who lost it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-v-holy-roman-emperor">Charles V</a> in 1543.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/charles-ii-duke-of-guelders.jpg" alt="Charles II, Duke of Guelders" width="262" height="276" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Karl_von_Egmond.JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
